<article class="env-lemma" id="0ASW">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0ASW">Lemma <span data-tag="0ASW">15.126.10</span>.</a> Let $R$ be a Bézout domain. Let $n \geq 1$ and $f_1, \ldots , f_ n \in R$ generate the unit ideal. There exists an invertible $n \times n$ matrix in $R$ whose first row is $f_1 \ldots f_ n$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      This follows from Lemma <a href="/tag/0ASU" data-tag="0ASU">15.126.8</a> but we can also prove it directly as follows. By induction on $n$. The result holds for $n = 1$. Assume $n > 1$. We may assume $f_1 \not= 0$ after renumbering. Choose $f \in R$ such that $(f) = (f_1, \ldots , f_{n - 1})$. Let $A$ be an $(n - 1) \times (n - 1)$ matrix whose first row is $f_1/f, \ldots , f_{n - 1}/f$. Choose $a, b \in R$ such that $af - bf_ n = 1$ which is possible because $1 \in (f_1, \ldots , f_ n) = (f, f_ n)$. Then a solution is the matrix 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \left( \begin{matrix}  f 

&  0 

&  \ldots 

&  0 

&  f_ n 

\\  0 

&  1 

&  \ldots 

&  0 

&  0 

\\ & &  \ldots 

\\  0 

&  0 

&  \ldots 

&  1 

&  0 

\\  b 

&  0 

&  \ldots 

&  0 

&  a 

\end{matrix} \right) \left( \begin{matrix} & & &  0 

\\ &  A 

\\ & & &  0 

\\  0 

&  \ldots 

&  0 

&  1 

\end{matrix} \right)  \]
</div>
    <p>
       Observe that the left matrix is invertible because it has determinant $1$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>